<section class="bg-base-100 text-base-content">
  <div class="mx-auto grid max-w-[84rem] gap-x-14 gap-y-12 px-5 pt-14 pb-20 md:px-8 md:pt-20 md:pb-28 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:items-start">
    <figure class="lg:col-span-5">
      <img src="/media/salesroom.jpg" class="w-full rounded-box">
      <figcaption class="mono mt-3 max-w-[52ch] text-[0.8125rem] text-base-content/80">A furniture salesroom in Grand Rapids in the 1870s, when the city's market was beginning. Not this building.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="lg:col-span-7">
      <h1 class="title-page">A sample floor, again</h1>
      <div class="reading mt-8 max-w-[60ch] text-[1.1875rem] text-base-content/80">
        <p>The Holloway Furniture Company built its works here in 1906 and kept the fourth floor for samples. Twice a year, in January and July, buyers from across the country walked between the season's chairs, desks and sideboards up here and wrote their orders. The company closed in 1962. The floor was a printer's stockroom, then empty for twenty years.</p>
        <p>Lena Brouwer had designed office desks and chairs for twelve years at one of the city's furniture makers, and knew what happens to samples: nobody sits at them. In 2019 she leased the floor, sanded the maple and filled it with floor models from local showrooms. Almost every desk here was a sample once. About sixty people work at them now.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-10 grid items-center gap-6 border-t border-base-300 pt-8 sm:grid-cols-[12rem_minmax(0,1fr)]">
        <img src="/media/desk-catalog.jpg" class="w-48 rounded-box">
        <p class="mono text-[0.8125rem] text-base-content/80">A desk from a Grand Rapids maker's catalog, about 1910. We have three of its grandchildren by the north windows.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
